yst_adjustment": -4.69, "key_factors": ["Phil Scott's cross-party incumbency: he has won Vermont by wide margins despite its strong Democratic lean and has historically outperformed his polls", "Expert ratings (six of eight call it Likely R) and prediction markets giving Janoo only about a 25% chance both favor Scott", "Thin, conflicting polling: one September survey shows Janoo up six, while others give Scott leads of three to fifteen points", "Bernie Sanders' endorsement of Janoo and the 2026 environment give Democrats a genuine but uphill opening"], "rationale": "Vermont's Democratic lean is offset by Scott's unusual personal popularity and record of running far ahead of his party, which raters and markets both respect. The lone survey showing Amanda Janoo ahead is an outlier against three recent polls giving Scott the lead, but the race's late energy and the state's blue fundamentals keep this short of Likely R.", "watch": "Another poll confirming Janoo's September numbers, or a clear shift in the race's tone after the debates, would move this toward tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Phil Scott (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican Governor Phil Scott seeks another term in a state that leans Democratic by double digits, facing Democrat Amanda Janoo, who has been endorsed by Bernie Sanders. Scott has repeatedly won by wide margins by building a cross-party coalition, and most analysts rate the race Likely R, with two calling it Lean R. Prediction markets give Janoo roughly a one-in-four chance.\n\nRecent polling is sparse and inconsistent: surveys from June through early October variously show Scott ahead by margins of three to fifteen points, while one September survey puts Janoo up six. Late-October debate coverage focused on healthcare, taxes, and school consolidation rather than any clear misstep by the incumbent. The forecast expects Scott to win by a moderate margin, with elevated uncertainty given the thin and conflicting polling